# microduck A/B disk layout (RAUC). The why, with measurements and source
# references, is in docs/agents/rauc.md; run scripts/check-wks-layout.py after any
# change -- it asserts the fixed offsets and the env triple, in 0.1 s.
#
#   part          offset      size
#   idblock        32 KiB    3552 KiB   no GPT entry; the bootrom reads sector 64
#   v_storage    3584 KiB     256 KiB   Rockchip vendor storage (MAC, serial)
#   uboot_env    4064 KiB      32 KiB   = 0x3F8000, the default environment
#   uboot           8 MiB       4 MiB
#   boot           16 MiB       4 MiB   U-Boot's own DTB, no kernel (each slot has its own)
#   (unallocated)  20 MiB      28 MiB
#   rootfs_a       48 MiB       4 GiB
#   rootfs_b     4144 MiB       4 GiB   same rootfs as A; RAUC rewrites it
#   data         8240 MiB       1 GiB   /var/lib/robot, survives updates; grown to the
#                                       end of the disk at boot (microduck-data-grow)
#                             ~9.1 GiB -- fits a 16 GB card.
#
# The slots are the ceiling for every OS update a shipped board will ever take: the
# table never changes after flashing. 4 GiB each, against ~0.4 GiB used in 2026-11,
# so there is room to grow. data starts small to keep the image small and quick to
# flash, and takes the rest of the eMMC (~54 GiB on a beta) on its first boot.
#
# The first 16 MiB are Rockchip's documented table, not a choice: vendor code
# writes vendor storage, the environment and U-Boot at fixed byte offsets without
# reading the GPT. 4..8 and 12..16 MiB stay empty as Rockchip reserves them.
#
# Every partition has an explicit --offset and --fixed-size, so wic fails rather
# than shifts: a bundle built against one layout must never write into another.
# Partitions are addressed by label everywhere but one: U-Boot's ext4load needs a
# number and this U-Boot has no `part number`, so its boot script finds rootfs_a and
# rootfs_b by their fixed partition GUIDs below (`part uuid`). That keeps one U-Boot
# right whatever the numbering -- 5/6 here, 6/7 on cards flashed while boot_b
# existed. scripts/check-wks-layout.py checks the GUIDs against the boot script.
# One line per part: wic's parser has no line continuation.

bootloader --ptable gpt

# --fixed-size here is a bound, not a size: with no GPT entry, nothing else would
# stop idblock growing into vendor storage.
part --source rawcopy --sourceparams="file=idblock.img" --align 32 --fixed-size 3552K --no-table

# Reserved so nothing of ours can ever land on it. U-Boot fills it on first boot.
part --offset 7168s --fixed-size 256K --fstype=none --part-name v_storage

# The default environment, not a hole: an unmapped range is one bmaptool never
# writes, so a reflashed card kept the old one -- its BOOT_ORDER, and its saved
# bootcmd, i.e. the old boot script (#24). u-boot-rockchip.bbappend builds it.
part --source rawcopy --sourceparams="file=u-boot-initial-env.bin" --offset 8128s --fixed-size 32K --part-name uboot_env

part --source rawcopy --sourceparams="file=uboot.img" --offset 8M --fixed-size 4M --part-name uboot

# U-Boot's own DTB, and nothing else. Vendor U-Boot sets itself up from the
# rk-kernel.dtb in this partition at init (init_kernel_dtb, before any boot script),
# found by the exact name `boot`. Without it, it falls back to a generic tree that
# cannot read the OTP: a fresh flash then has no serial-number and a Wi-Fi MAC that
# changes every boot (beta, 2026-11-10). boot.img has no kernel (the kernel recipe
# builds it so): U-Boot boots only the slot's, and a slot without one is given up
# on (the U-Boot patch). ~0.2 MiB used. 20..48 MiB stays unallocated, so the slots
# keep their offsets.
part --source rawcopy --sourceparams="file=boot.img" --offset 16M --fixed-size 4M --part-name boot

# The kernel's root= comes from U-Boot's boot script (root=PARTLABEL=rootfs_a|b).
# The GUIDs are how that script finds each slot's partition (above), and they keep
# two builds of one commit byte-identical. rootfs_a's is meta-rockchip's
# RK_ROOTDEV_UUID default, written out so the check can compare it.
part / --source rootfs --offset 48M --fixed-size 4096M --fstype=ext4 --part-name rootfs_a --uuid 614e0000-0000-4b53-8000-1d28000054a9 --mkfs-extraopts "${RK_ROOTFS_EXTRAOPTS}"
#
# rootfs_b gets the same rootfs as rootfs_a, so a freshly flashed board has two
# bootable slots. U-Boot's script falls back to B once A has used its attempts
# (three boots without rauc-mark-good, e.g. three quick reboots). With B empty it
# used to fall back to the `boot` partition, whose kernel panicked on an empty
# root -- the board looked bricked until the counters wrapped (first beta board,
# 2026-10-06). Without that partition an empty B is skipped, but a fresh board
# should still have two slots that boot. Costs one more write at flash time.
part --source rootfs --offset 4144M --fixed-size 4096M --fstype=ext4 --part-name rootfs_b --uuid 614e0001-0000-4b53-8000-1d28000054a9 --mkfs-extraopts "${RK_ROOTFS_EXTRAOPTS}"

# User data: policies, sounds, configd state, wifi profiles. Seeded from the image's
# own /var/lib/robot, because the mount hides whatever the rootfs has there; wic
# keeps ownership across --change-directory. Only a full reflash re-seeds it, so a
# later image's new files here never reach a board that already has one.
# Mounted by recipes-core/microduck-data, not --mountpoint, so a missing partition
# is skipped rather than failing the daemons that need it. It must stay the last
# partition: microduck-data-grow only ever grows the last one.
part --source rootfs --change-directory=var/lib/robot --offset 8240M --fixed-size 1024M --fstype=ext4 --label data --part-name data --mkfs-extraopts "-F -i 8192 -b 4096"
